{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Israel loved, etc.</b> He prefaced by giving the reason for the brothers' hatred toward their younger brother, and said <b>and Israel</b>, etc. — meaning that although Joseph brought an evil report about his brothers, nevertheless no harm resulted, for the brothers would argue with him and rebuke him, and their jealousy would subside. But because this additional factor was added — that Israel loved him, etc., more than all his brothers, meaning more than all of them together — and he also publicized the love and made it known to them by making him the striped tunic, which declares that they knew, and their father knew that they knew — as it says <b>and his brothers saw that</b>, etc. — from that point on, when hatred came there was no hope of rectifying the situation and making peace. And this is what it says <b>and they hated him</b> — for the reasons of his prior actions, that he brought an evil report about them and they could not, etc. — because seeing that he was loved by his father more than all of them, there was no possibility of arguing with him, for his words would be vindicated and not theirs; and since they could not confront him with open rebuke that would shame him for what he had done so that he would never again do this thing, there was no possibility of getting him to change. And this is what it says <b>and they could not speak peaceably</b> — with difficulty — <b>to peace</b>, meaning to make peace between them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:21.487484943Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}